  \left| 1 + e^{2 \pi i k / n} \right|^n = \begin{cases} 
      2^n, & \text{if $k = 0$ (mod $n$)} \\
      1, & \text{otherwise}.
   \end{cases}
\]Therefore, \[
  \left| \left( 1 + e^{2 \pi i k / n} \right)^n + 1 \right| = \begin{cases} 
      3, & \text{if $k = 0$ (mod $n$)} \\
      2, & \text{if $k \ne 0$ (mod $n$)}.
   \end{cases}
\]It follows that the given product is zero if and only if $n = 1.$  (If $n > 1,$ each term in the product has magnitude at least $1,$ so their product cannot be zero.)  We conclude that the only solution is $n = 1,$ so the number of integer solutions is $\boxed{1}.$</think> <answer> 1</answer>

